WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.820
Would you work with us in terms of what regulations
you think are necessary in your industry?

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:05.620
Absolutely.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.260
Mark Zuckerberg is the multi-billionaire founder
and CEO of Facebook,

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.140
and this week he testified before Congress,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:17.120
assuring lawmakers that his company will play
nice with government regulators.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.380
Ladies and gentlemen, Richard Hendricks!

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.320
Richard Hendricks from HBO’s Silicon Valley

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.940
is the bumbling CEO of the unfortunately named
Pied Piper.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.120
His memorable moments include evacuating his
bowels, vomiting,

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.820
and then lunging into a glass wall in front of all of his workers.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.420
One is poised when being grilled congress,

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.340
the other can’t even deliver a pep talk
to his staff without hurling under his desk.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.480
But our best hope for the future of the internet

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.520
is with Richard Hendricks and not Mark Zuckerberg,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:48.360
and here’s why.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.520
We need to take a more proactive role and
a broader view of our responsibility.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.360
It's not enough to just build tools, we need
to make sure that they're used for good.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:01.880
In his testimony, Zuckerberg welcomed regulation,
and even agreed to help craft it.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.860
Mark Zuckerberg is in the same position as
the late-nineteenth century railroad tycoons.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.869
Contrary to conventional wisdom, these robber
barons embraced regulation

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:13.940
as a way to raise the barriers to entry for
competitors

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.820
who were eating into their profits and market
share.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.000
Still sporting a hoodie, Richard Hendricks
is at an earlier stage of his career.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.659
He’s trying to build a new internet in an
effort to outmaneuver Hooli,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.780
a fictional amalgamation of Google and Facebook.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.140
Hooli is the future...

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:34.020
Even Richard’s specific approach to disrupting
Facebook is more than just TV fantasy.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.660
We could build a completely decentralized
version of our current internet.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:45.160
With no firewalls, no tolls, no government
regulation, no spying.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:49.800
Information would be totally free in every
sense of the word.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.399
Richard represents the next wave of innovation—

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.880
the competitor who, if congress and the government stays out of it,

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.840
will eventually erode Facebook’s market
share by offering a better product.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.340
When you add more rules that companies need
to follow

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.920
that's something that a larger company like
ours inherently just has the resources to go do

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.900
and that might just be harder for a smaller
company getting started to be able to comply with.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:16.000
There’s a real world movement in the tech
world to build a new decentralized web

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.920
that would give users actual control over
their own data,

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:24.840
and create open platforms that aren’t controlled
by any single all-powerful CEO.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.340
One reason to bet on these real life projects
to decentralize the internet,

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.780
projects like Blockstack and Ethereum,

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.560
is that talented engineers are beating down
their doors,

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:38.160
because working at Google and Facebook is
lucrative but it's also soul killing.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:47.260
We've been given the opportunity to rewrite
the most important thing in human history

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:51.280
and we can build it the way it should have
been all along,

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.400
you know, not this shitty addictive parasite

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.520
that companies like Hooli use to spy on us
exploit us.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.680
As Facebook and Congress start to write new
rules for cyberspace,

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.860
all of us who believe in free expression and
permission-less innovation

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.880
have a real stake in making sure that the
future of the internet

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.860
remains as wide open and uncharted as possible.

